On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 09:58:53 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Good idea but this seems needlessly complex. Probably it can grep for
> lines that begin with subject:, pick the first one (presuambly that's
> the subject header line, right?) and then read everything after it.

Only if the search stops at the end of the header. A body or a quoted
message can carry its own "Subject:" line, so v2 reads up to the first
blank line and nothing past it.

Taking just the one line falls short, though. Long subjects are folded
onto a second line; git format-patch and notmuch reply both do it, and
one message on the list already has one. A plain grep would commit
it as "mail: Re: [PATCH 3/3] claude: Let process-email archive what
needs no", without the last word. So v2 joins folded lines first,
with a one-line awk, and then does your grep, as
sed -n 's/^[Ss]ubject: *//p'.

> Same with detecting added. Probably it can `jj st maildir` and then use
> awk to identify added ones and only print their names.

Done, as you describe: jj st maildir | awk '$1 == "A" { print $2 }'.
Renames show as "R" and edits as "M", so both stay in the working
copy.

> I'm not opposed to this I guess... there is something nice about doing
> it all in jj I suppose. It just seems like it's doing a lot there that
> could be done with more idiomatic shell script processing.

Agreed. The recipe is down from 17 lines to 10, and each step is one
pipeline. It is posted as "[PATCH v2] mail: Add just commit-mail" [1].
The notes below its --- line say how it was tested.

Thanks for the review.

